4.2.2 Public Sector  
The public sector is a key area for advancing digital competencies in the public sector, which are essential for:  
• defining and systematizing key competencies across various professional profiles and roles assumed during the implementation of the digital transformation (IT specialists, managers, and public employees);  
• initiating needs assessment and training interventions;  
• promoting the strengthening of digital skills through personnel turnover.

Strategia Nazionale per le Competenze Digitali, Release 1.0  
4.2 Initiatives in Progress  
4.2.1 Private Sector Unemployed Individuals  
The Public Administration, within the framework of a comprehensive system-wide strategy aimed at supporting the national business sector in capturing opportunities offered by technological innovation, has deployed a set of tools designed to:  
• enhance digital competencies;  
• direct businesses toward technological transformation;  
• disseminate innovation across all levels;  
• bridge the gap between research and industry;  
• support demand for innovative technological solutions.  

Similarly, within the private sector, increasing attention is being paid to issues related to training and process or product innovation. Furthermore:  
• In collaboration with enterprises undergoing digital transformation of their business processes, an active role is played through a close public-private synergy by the Ministry of Economic Development (MiSE). In parallel to Competence Centers, which perform functions supporting technology transfer,

• planning and management of targeted training programmes on digital issues applied to public administration, including structured evaluation of progress achieved;  
• promotion of dialogue with the research and business sectors on various aspects of digital transformation, in order to create opportunities for organizational learning and foster talent retention.  

4.4 Impact and indicators  

4.4.1 Private sector not employed  

In relation to the private sector, the impact intended to be produced, consistent with the expected results of the Strategy, may be summarized as follows:  
• increase in the number of private employees with basic digital competencies;  
• increase in the number of private employees with specialized digital competencies;  
• modernization of production processes;  
• greater transfer of technology to businesses;  
• increased presence of the business world in education and vice versa;  
• greater interaction between digital enterprises and traditional enterprises;  
• multiplication of national initiatives in emerging technologies such as Blockchain, IoT, and Artificial Intelligence.

National Strategy for Digital Competence, Release 1.0
Concerns the efficiency of public administration, both for the competitiveness of the economy as a whole. The lack of digitalization has profound effects on growth and productivity;
• if it is considered that ICT workers currently represent 4% of the workforce, the country's major challenge will be to support new actors, specialists, and their strategic skills in their emergence on the market, while simultaneously enhancing the digital competencies of the remaining 96% of non-ICT workers, thereby strengthening the broad-based entrepreneurial sector. This figure is even more significant if it is considered that only a portion of ICT workers engage in technical development, and even fewer are employed in sectors developing or utilizing frontier technologies;
• therefore, it becomes evident the need to support the entrepreneurial sector with actions aimed at supporting on one hand the technological transformation of their respective business models and on the other the training of personnel.

• adaptation of the delivery methods of education through the use of ICT technologies and the development of new services for students (cooperative work, availability of online materials, support to student assistance, support to the provision of in-person teaching interventions);  
• modification and adaptation of the current training pathways to meet the needs of professions affected by digital transformation;  
• strengthening of training programmes strongly oriented towards industrial research and innovation;  
• enhancement of human capital and infrastructure for researchers dedicated to teaching and scientific activities related to ICT and its applications;  
• adaptation of analysis models for observatories.  
