Within the general interventions, some actions can be implemented quickly:
• Implement an important and priority evolution in the training pathways to favour, at all levels, the study and application of ICT methodologies, approaches and technologies combined with the specificity of different applied domains;
• Strengthen the culture of business models and management based on the use of ICT technologies, as well as

• to draw on experiences (both from the public and private sectors and civil society) that have reached a level of maturity sufficient to serve as a reference;  
• to address the systemic problem of the decline in integration at the territorial level, beyond the scope of different types of authorities and administrations, and its episodic nature in interventions and initiatives.  

Therefore, the Operational Plan must include organic, system-wide, and national measures that:  
• take into account experiences and initiatives that have proven effective at local and national levels, while also considering the dialogue with other key actors in the Community landscape and the digital competence plans promoted by the Commission itself;  
• tackle the development of digital skills in a differentiated manner according to the level of each Member State, thereby identifying gradual objectives and targeted actions, so as to engage those who play a mediating and facilitatory role towards citizens in different areas and where they are best positioned to do so.

• facilitate the access to the use of digital telecommunications services in the ultra-long distance and the digital technologies.  
The specific indicators are linked to intervention priorities and lines of action that are intended to be pursued, as previously highlighted, namely:  
• improvement of the technological skills of the workforce;  
• technological improvement of production processes;  
• strengthening of the link between education and industry;  
• improvement in access to ultra-long distance networks and the use of emerging technologies.  
