• make the public administration more attractive than high-potential resources in areas related to innovation and digitalization;
• promote the creation of a shared culture around innovation and digitization across all levels of administration and enhance the professional skills of those already working in the public sector.
The priorities identified are translated into the following lines of action:
• recruitment of managers who, in addition to usual requirements, possess digital competencies, transversal skills, and the ability to solve complex problems;
• career guidance pathways and specialist training on digitalization in collaboration with the university system;
• hiring procedures for non-managerial staff that include verification of necessary qualifications to work in a more digitally oriented public administration, paying particular attention to specific professional needs and sector-specific characteristics (e.g., health, justice, infrastructure and transport, etc.).

In conclusion, the strategic approach of the Ministry of Education, even with the vision of promoting autonomy for school subjects, has been for a long time focused - through the activation of partnerships that constitute exemplary examples of collaboration between schools, universities, non-profit associations and industry sectors - on the promotion of initiatives aimed at strengthening the digital competencies of students, supporting the development of both soft skills and basic digital skills as well as specific ICT sector skills.

3.2.2 Universities and Higher Education
Among the institutional initiatives that seek to create training pathways integrating ICT skills and domain-specific competencies, it is observed that:
• there is a minimum reliance on "professionalization degrees," currently not considered suitable and poorly correlated with other degrees;
• for the economic business sector, in some computer science departments, Computer Science Degree courses (CdS) have been created where computer science and economic culture are combined within the same pathway;
• recently announced or currently underway are three-year undergraduate courses in the economic business sector with a strong ICT and AI connotation.

In particular, for the CPIA, in addition to training interventions for teachers, priority is given to direct measures aimed at promoting the occasional use of technological tools and laboratory spaces not only within expansion projects of the educational offer but also in first-level regulatory pathways, as well as in language learning pathways. With regard to this latter type of educational offer, the availability of teaching modules to be carried out through the support of technological tools would prove particularly effective, even taking into account the wide range of potential beneficiaries.

6.4 Impact and indicators

The impact intended to be achieved, consistent with the expected results of the Strategy, can be summarized as follows:
- increase in citizens and active population with basic digital competencies;
- increase among disadvantaged individuals (including elderly persons) with basic digital competencies;
- increase in the use of the internet, with particular reference to essential activities (including eGovernment services) and vulnerable categories.

• the establishment of laboratories and, more generally, workspaces where researchers, students, and professionals from diverse cultural and disciplinary backgrounds can engage with one another, thereby fostering the development and incubation of innovative projects with an entrepreneurial orientation. The creation of these spaces should involve collaboration between universities and the industrial system and be designed for both in-person and remote use;
• a constant link to the school system and the industrial system to improve the complex cycle of training, guidance, and lifelong learning, while developing, through university-industry partnerships, new forms of face-to-face teaching that integrate advanced digital services;
• extending the analysis of observatories beyond the level of required competencies and the types of training, as already done, to the forms in which non-curricular training pathways are developed.

Interventions on current educational offerings will focus on:

The lack of digital competence is one of the main obstacles to the development of Italy and constitutes a priority:  
• it has a negative impact both on the supply of digital services by the public and private sectors, as well as on access and enjoyment by citizens;  
• it exposes a significant part of the population to the risk of social exclusion and from the labour market;  
• it hinders access to forms of public participation and consultation;  
• it increases the risk of exposure of citizens to misinformation at large scale. 1  
An inadequate level of digital competence does not concern only the private lives of citizens, but also has an impact on their employability, on their access to the digital environment for continuous knowledge and skills updates. A data point that deserves attention concerns also workers at risk due to automation processes: only 20% of them benefit from continuing training.  
